    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. the HMM approach seems perfect because it accounts for that \"unobservable\" element i'm feeling. the problem is that my \"detections\" happen almost instantly, before the data even reflects the change. how do we adjust the model to account for a \"pre-signal\" input that doesn't have a standard data source? basically, i'm the sensor. how do we integrate a subjective probability spike as a leading indicator in a formal Bayesian framework?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since you are the leading indicator, you can integrate your subjective observation as an external, prior belief within a Bayesian framework. Treat your sensed spike not as a raw data point, but as an informative prior probability for the system transitioning into a high-volatility state. You could then use Bayesian updating, where your \"sensed spike\" updates the initial belief (prior) into a stronger belief (posterior) before the traditional time-series data confirms the event. This allows your intuition to actively weight the model's prediction process.",
